# Line endings.
#
# Git has been warning "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on every commit in this
# repo. That is core.autocrlf doing its default Windows thing: store LF, hand
# the working tree CRLF. Harmless while everything runs on Windows, and not
# harmless at all once these files are copied into a Linux container, where a
# CRLF in a shell script produces errors like "/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter"
# that name a file which looks perfectly fine in an editor.
#
# eol=lf settles it: LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on
# every platform. Modern Windows editors -- VS Code included -- handle LF
# without complaint, and the files that end up in a Docker image are then
# byte-identical to the ones committed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must never be touched. Listing them explicitly protects them
# even if the detection above ever guesses wrong.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary

# Cached API responses are data, not source. They are JSON so the rule above
# would already normalise them, which is fine -- this is here to say that is
# deliberate, so a diff on a cache file is never a line-ending diff.
server/cache/*.json text eol=lf
